        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Jadidism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s Muslim and Turkic regions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkortostan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> (Turkestan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, Samarqand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>), with echoes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The first new-method school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> (1884); new-method schools appearing in the last decade of the 19th century; a burst of Turkic newspapers after the 1905 Revolution until 1908; over 5,000 Jadid schools by 1916; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> government (1917–1918); alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in the early 1920s; purges from 1926 through the 1920s and 1930s.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ismail Gasprinsky (Gaspirali), Crimean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> educator and publisher; Mahmud Khoja Behbudiy, playwright and school founder; Abdurrauf Fitrat; Cholpan; Munawwar Qari; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni">Sadriddin Ayni</a>; Musa Bigiev; Ghabdennasir Qursawi; Zaynulla Rasulev.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A network of new-method schools and a Turkic-language press, but no equality under Russian rule; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> government was crushed in 1917–1918, and the Jadids were purged under Stalin. They have since been rehabilitated as national heroes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
