    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Jadids, who called themselves progressives, intellectuals or simply youth, held that Muslim peoples in Tsarist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had fallen into moral and societal decay that only a new kind of knowledge and European-modelled reform could cure.</li><li>Their signature was the Usul-i Jadid, the new method of teaching, which added geography, history, mathematics and science to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuttab">maktab</a> curriculum, taught reading by phonetic methods and spread through schools and a Turkic-language press.</li><li>Their rivals were the Qadimist clergy and, after 1917, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, who first used them and then purged them as counterrevolutionary agents; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> has since honoured them as national heroes.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Samarqand bookstore of 1914 sold books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a>, Ottoman, Arabic and Persian, along with dictionaries, atlases, maps and globes, according to the historian Adeeb Khalid.</p></aside>
    </section>

