    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1884</span><span class="rf-text">Ismail Gaspirali founds the first new-method school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text">The 1905 Revolution opens a period in which Turkic-language newspapers appear in Turkestan; Russian authorities forbid them again in 1908</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">A school for training teachers in Jadid methods is founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> by Heyder Sayrani and the merchant Mukhsut Muhiti</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1916</span><span class="rf-text">Jadid-run institutions of learning number over 5,000</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">Jadids found a provisional government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-soviet">Tashkent Soviet</a> crushes in 1918, killing around 14,000 people</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">The Communist Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> turns on the Jadids in the same purges that struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930s</span><span class="rf-text">Virtually the entire intelligentsia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, including Cholpan and Abdurrauf Fitrat, is purged</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni.jpg" alt="Sadriddin Ayni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni">Sadriddin Ayni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AyniSadriddin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Jadid movement arose among the Turkic Muslim peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the late 19th century. Its members held that these peoples had entered a period of moral and societal decay. They saw the traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuttab">maktab</a>, the primary school, as the clearest sign of stagnation. Pupils memorised religious texts rather than having them explained: the Tajik Jadid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadriddin-ayni">Sadriddin Ayni</a>, who attended a maktab in the 1890s, recalled that he learned the Arabic alphabet as an aid to memorisation but could not read a text he had not already memorised.</p><p>The Russian government offered an alternative. From 1884 it set up “Russo-native” schools in Turkestan, which combined Russian language and history with maktab-like instruction by native teachers, many of whom were Jadids. These schools reached too few people to bring the cultural renewal the Jadids wanted; in 1916 fewer than 300 Turks attended Russian higher primary schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p>Russian rule also kept the populations apart. Residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, the capital of Turkestan, was limited to Russian elites, and most cities had separate quarters for Russians and “natives”. New technologies of communication and transport, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, the postal system and railways, carried Jadid ideals across Central Asia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Jadids advocated an Islamic social and cultural reformation through the revival of pristine Islamic beliefs and teachings, while engaging with modernity. Education was a principal aim. They wanted schools that would teach differently from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuttab">maktabs</a>, and held that the traditional system produced graduates without the skills to navigate the modern world. Their new-method schools, the Usul-i Jadid from which the name Jadidism derives, kept the traditional subjects and added geography, history, mathematics and science. Probably the most important change was teaching children to read by phonetic methods, which did more to encourage functional literacy.</p><p>Jadid schools focused on literacy in the native, often Turkic, languages rather than Russian or Arabic, taught “Islamic history and methods of thought” rather than memorisation alone, did not allow corporal punishment and encouraged girls to attend, although few parents sent their daughters. Some classrooms had benches, desks, blackboards and maps.</p><p>Jadids also criticised local society. Central Asian Jadids accused its leaders of permitting moral decay, as seen in alcoholism, pederasty, polygamy and gender discrimination, and of cooperating with Russian officials to cement their authority. Anti-colonial discourse was a major strand, and leaders such as Gasprinsky promoted anti-Russian political activism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/october-manifesto.jpg" alt="October Manifesto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Repin_17October.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Repin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1884 Ismail Gaspirali founded the first new-method school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. Such schools quickly gained prominence among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, popularised by thinkers such as Ghabdennasir Qursawi, Musa Bigiev and Gaspirali himself, but spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> more slowly and sporadically. Munawwar Qari founded Central Asia's first school on the Jadid model in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. Gasprinsky's newspaper Tercüman (“Interpreter”) was widely read in all Turkic regions of the empire. From 1905 to 1917, 166 new Tatar-language newspapers and magazines appeared. The first Turkic-language newspaper produced in Turkestan dates from after the 1905 revolution, and Central Asians published many papers until the Russian authorities forbade them again in 1908.</p><p>Russian electoral rules limited Jadid political power. After the 1905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a>, Turkestan's population was divided into “native” and “non-native” franchises, each sending one representative to the Duma, which gave the “non-native” franchise a two-thirds majority although it made up less than ten percent of the population. In 1913 a teacher-training institution in Jadid methods opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>. By 1916 Jadid-run institutions of learning numbered over 5,000.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 the Jadids founded a unified provisional government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, intending to remain autonomous from the USSR. It lasted a year, 1917–1918, before the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-soviet">Tashkent Soviet</a> brutally crushed it; around 14,000 people, including many leading Jadids, were killed in the ensuing massacre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra.jpg" alt="Muhammad Amin Bughra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra">Muhammad Amin Bughra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Amin_Bughra2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A leading figure in educational reform was the Crimean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> intellectual, educator, publisher and politician Ismail Gasprinsky (1851–1914). Mahmud Khoja Behbudiy, author of the play The Patricide and founder of one of Turkestan's first Jadid schools, carried Gasprinsky's ideas back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Among the Tatars, Ghabdennasir Qursawi and Musa Bigiev helped popularise the new schools, and the Bashkir leader Zaynulla Rasulev organised one of the first Jadid madrasahs. The Bukharan author Abdurrauf Fitrat criticised the clergy for discouraging the modernisation he thought necessary to protect Central Asia from Russian incursions, and the Tatar socialist Ismail Abidiy published newspapers in Central Asia.</p><p>Jadids had much in common with their opponents. Many were educated in traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuttab">maktabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and came from privileged families. Adeeb Khalid sees Jadids and the Qadimist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a> as engaged in a battle over which values should be projected onto Central Asian culture. Many clergy opposed the Jadids, decrying their programmes as un-Islamic, heretical innovations, while Jadids called the Qadimists corrupt, self-interested elites. The Jadids also condemned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> as proponents of a popular, unorthodox form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>Jadids were not of one mind. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra">Muhammad Amin Bughra</a> and Masud Sabri rejected the name “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a>” for the Turkic people there, and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> opposed the single Turkestani language the Jadids proposed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Jadids failed to achieve equality under Russian imperial rule in Turkestan. The Russians treated them better than the clergy, whom they regarded as dangerous extremists, because of the progressive and secular nature of their reforms, yet kept the idea that Turkestan's people should have separate living spaces and limited voting rights. Their papers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> usually had small circulations and needed patronage to survive, and the Russian censors saw them as potentially subversive.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> at first offered state-funded schools, a print sphere immune to market forces and new organs of political authority, and by the early 1920s large numbers of Jadids joined the Communist Party. The Bolsheviks used these facilities on their own terms, for political education and propaganda. The Jadids hoped for a Turkic nation, while the Bolsheviks envisioned a Central Asia divided on ethnographic lines. The Bolsheviks then built local cadres bound to socialist revolutionism and cut off from Turkic cultural practice, who displaced the Jadids from public life.</p><p>By 1926 the Communist Party felt secure enough to act against traditional authorities without the Jadids, who were denounced as the mouthpiece of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and counterrevolutionary agents to be stripped of their jobs, arrested and executed if necessary. Through the 1920s and 1930s virtually the entire intelligentsia of Central Asia was purged, among them the writers Cholpan and Abdurrauf Fitrat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jadids have now been rehabilitated as “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> National Heroes” in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, where they were recognised and honoured after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a>. Jadid-like schools were built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> Progress Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> after 1934, and Gasprinsky's example inspired figures such as Burhan Shahidi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. The Jadids popularised the identity of “Turkestani”, and Muhammad Geldiev, a Jadid, influenced the formulation of literary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>, whose creation was assigned to a commission in 1921.</p><p>The movement carried an anti-colonial critique beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After the defeat and collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the aftermath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, the Jadids extended their critiques to Allied powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and other Western European empires.</p><p>There were substantial ideological differences within the movement. Persian was the language of Jadidists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> at the start of the 1900s, and there was no broad scheme or ideology of pan-Turkism among them. Jadids normally referred to themselves by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> terms Taraqqiparvarlar (“progressives”), Ziyalilar (“intellectuals”) or Yäşlär/Yoshlar (“youth”). What marked Jadids as a group was their widespread use of print media and their advocacy of the new method of teaching.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Jadidism was a Turco-Islamic modernist movement among Muslims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, built around new-method schools, a Turkic-language press and Islamic reform. Russian rule limited it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> crushed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> government in 1917–1918 and the Soviet purges removed its intelligentsia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> now honours its members as national heroes.</em></p>

